        heading: 'Part One: The world of Oceania',
        body:
          'Winston Smith is thirty-nine years old, employed in the Records Department of the Ministry of Truth, and paid to make the past behave. His job is to revise old newspapers so that every prediction the Party made turns out to have been correct, dropping the inconvenient originals into a slot called the memory hole, where they burn. Orwell uses these opening chapters to set out the rules of Oceania: the telescreen that watches while it talks, the Two Minutes Hate directed at the traitor Emmanuel Goldstein, the war that never ends, and the three slogans on the white face of the Ministry, "WAR IS PEACE, FREEDOM IS SLAVERY, IGNORANCE IS STRENGTH."\n\nWinston commits his first crime quietly, sitting in a shallow alcove that the telescreen cannot quite see, when he opens a blank book bought on impulse in a junk shop and writes "DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER." He knows precisely what he has done. The Thought Police will find him eventually, and the only question is how long the interval will be. In the same diary he sets down the sentence that becomes his creed: if there is hope, it lies in the proles.\n\nTwo figures begin to shape his fate. One is a dark-haired girl from the Fiction Department whom he first suspects of spying on him and privately wishes dead. The other is O’Brien, an Inner Party official whose glance across a corridor Winston reads, on almost no evidence at all, as a signal of secret agreement. He wanders the prole districts where the Party barely bothers to watch, and in Mr. Charrington’s shop he buys a lump of glass with a scrap of coral sealed inside it, an object with no use whatsoever, which is exactly why he wants it.',
