          'In the windowless cells of the Ministry of Love, Winston is starved and beaten and then handed over to O’Brien, who conducts the interrogation with the patience of a doctor and the certainty of a priest. O’Brien is not after a confession, since the Party already has all the confessions it could ever need. He wants Winston cured, which means remade from the inside, and so the torture is aimed at reason itself. Holding up four fingers, he demands that Winston not merely say five but genuinely see five, because a mind that can still be argued with is a mind still partly free.\n\nOne small reservation survives every stage of the treatment: Winston has not betrayed Julia in his heart. Room 101 exists to take that last thing from him. A cage of starving rats is fitted toward his face, and he screams "Do it to Julia!", offering up the one person he swore never to give away. The Party has not simply broken him; it has arranged for him to break himself, which is a different and far more complete kind of victory.\n\nReleased, he drifts through a hollow afterlife of Victory Gin and chess problems at the Chestnut Tree Café. He meets Julia once, in a cold park, and they admit without much feeling that each betrayed the other and that something inside them changed when they did. The last image is of Winston listening to a bulletin of victory, weeping with gratitude, having won the struggle against himself at last. He loves Big Brother.',
