    aboutAuthor:
      'George Orwell was the pen name of Eric Arthur Blair (1903 to 1950), an English novelist, essayist, and reporter who called himself a democratic socialist and who spent his career attacking authoritarianism wherever he found it, on the right and on the left alike. He was born in India, educated at Eton on a scholarship among boys far richer than himself, and then served for roughly five years as a colonial policeman in Burma, work he came to regard with shame and later turned into *Burmese Days* and the essay "Shooting an Elephant." Back in Europe he lived deliberately among tramps and dishwashers in order to write *Down and Out in Paris and London*, and he went down coal mines in the north of England for *The Road to Wigan Pier*.\n\nThe decisive experience was Spain. Orwell went to fight fascism in the Spanish Civil War, was shot through the throat, and then watched political factions on his own side rewrite events he had personally witnessed, producing newspaper accounts of battles that had never happened and denunciations of comrades who had betrayed no one. *Homage to Catalonia* records that disillusionment, and the memory of history being manufactured in front of him sits at the center of everything *1984* has to say about truth.\n\nHe wrote the novel on the remote Scottish island of Jura while seriously ill with the tuberculosis that would kill him, typing the final draft himself because he could not persuade a typist to come to the island. It was published in 1949, and he died in London the following January at the age of forty-six. The title is usually explained as a reversal of the last two digits of 1948, the year he finished the manuscript. In his essay "Why I Write," Orwell said his ambition was "to make political writing into an art," and this book is the fullest expression of that aim, addressed not to distant dictatorships but to the democracies he feared were capable of the same thing.',
