    aboutAuthor:
      'Harper Lee (1926 to 2016) grew up in Monroeville, Alabama, and wrote her novel out of the town she knew best. Like Scout she was a tomboy with a lawyer father, and like Scout she had a small, imaginative boy for a summer neighbor: Truman Capote, who became the model for Dill and remained her friend for decades, later taking her with him to Kansas to help with the reporting that produced *In Cold Blood*. The Alabama of her childhood was also the Alabama of racially charged trials such as the Scottsboro case, and the atmosphere surrounding Tom Robinson’s trial comes out of that world rather than out of invention.\n\nShe studied law for a time without finishing, moved to New York, and worked as an airline reservation clerk while writing, until friends gave her a year’s wages as a Christmas gift so that she could write full time. *To Kill a Mockingbird* was published in 1960, won the Pulitzer Prize, and made her famous almost overnight, after which she gave very few interviews and published no second novel for the rest of her working life. She returned to Monroeville and guarded her privacy with some determination.\n\nIn 2015, shortly before her death, a second book appeared. *Go Set a Watchman* was in fact an earlier draft, featuring an older and considerably more prejudiced Atticus, and its publication was accompanied by public argument over whether Lee had genuinely consented to it in her final years. The book complicated the saintly image of her hero, which some readers found painful and others found clarifying. Lee herself once said that she had only ever wanted to be "the Jane Austen of south Alabama," chronicling a small-town world with honesty about both its tenderness and its injustice.',
